-- A closure that cannot cross a pool boundary should not be built as though it
-- could. This program has ONE pool, so no closure in it can ever be sent
-- anywhere; the lambda captures nothing and its manifold gets no dispatch
-- entry, which is the compiler's own statement that it has no identity to
-- carry. It is nevertheless handed to a sourced higher-order function, whose
-- parameter is an ordinary `F: Fn` -- a position that accepts a plain closure.
--
-- Emitting a reference-counted trait object there costs an allocation and a
-- virtual call per element, in the one case where the machinery buys nothing.
-- The checks below read the generated pool because the cost is invisible in
-- the program's output: the answer is right either way. They count the vtable
-- and the allocation rather than any particular spelling of the good path.

module main (viaMap)

import root-rust

source Rust from "bench.rs" ("iota", "sum_vec" as sumVec)

iota :: Int -> [Int]
sumVec :: [Int] -> Int

viaMap :: Int -> Int
viaMap n = sumVec (map (\x -> x * 2) (iota n))
